  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 403 PM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThis afternoon there is an upper-level ridge over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. It will be hot this afternoon \nacross North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. An upper-\nlevel shortwave will move through the upper-level ridge and bring\nshowers and thunderstorms to the area this afternoon and evening.\nSee the Forecast Confidence & Scenarios section for details. On \nFriday the upper-level ridge begins to move east away from North- \ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana. As a result of the \nshortwave that moved through it will be slightly cooler across the\narea on Friday. Showers and thunderstorms will linger through \nmuch of the day on Friday across Southwestern Montana. Due to an \nincreasing pressure gradient and approaching upper-level trough it\nwill be breezy across North-central Montana on Friday. \n\nOn Saturday an upper-level trough associated with a Pacific Cold \nFront begins to move through North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. This brings cooler temperatures to the area\nwith showers and thunderstorms. It will also be breezy on \nSaturday due to a strong pressure gradient. On Sunday the upper-\nlevel trough moves over North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. This will cool temperatures down more to well-below \nseasonal averages with another day of showers and thunderstorms. \nOn Monday an upper-level ridge builds back in over North-central,\nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow temperatures \nto warm back up with mostly dry weather. Tuesday through Wednesday\nan upper-level trough will move back over the area which will \nbring showers and thunderstorms back to the area on Tuesday and \nWednesday. -IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nToday the Storm Prediction Center (SPC) issued a Slight Risk (15%\nchance for winds gusts of 58 mph or greater) for severe \nthunderstorms across North-central Montana between I-90 and US \nHighway 2 west of Fergus and Blaine Counties. Across the rest of \nNorth-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana the SPC issued a\nMarginal Risk (5% chance for wind gusts of 58 mph or greater) for\nsevere thunderstorms. This afternoon there will be deep \ninverted-V soundings with DCAPE values in excess of 1200 J/kg. The\nuncertainty with these storms this afternoon and evening is how \nthe current line of storms moving through North-central Montana \nwill affect the environment and if there will be clearing this \nafternoon before the next round of storms moves in to maximize the\nsevere potential of any storms that form. If there is sufficient \nclearing then the strongest storms have the potential to produce \noutflow boundaries that produce wind gusts up to 75 mph. \n\nOn Friday there is a 15 - 30% chance for thunder in Southwestern \nMontana southeast of a Bozeman to Dillon line with the highest \nchances in the West Yellowstone area. On Saturday there is a 25 - \n40% chance for thunder along and south of the Montana Highway 200 \nCorridor between Lincoln and Lewistown. On Saturday there is \ngreater than a 60% chance for a quarter inch of rain or greater \nacross Central and Southwestern Montana east of I-15. On Sunday \nacross North-central Montana east of I-15 there is a 40 - 60% \nchance for a quarter inch of rain or greater. On Sunday there is a\n50 - 70% chance for wind gusts of 40 mph or greater across most \nof North-central Montana. -IG",
